raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "The paper investigates whether the use of stigmatizing labels like 'convicted criminal' in Democratic campaign ads against Trump spills over to worsen Democrats' perceptions of individuals with criminal records more broadly, and whether a counter-narrative can mitigate these negative effects.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "To test for the possibility of stigmatizing language spillover, this preliminary study employs a survey experiment testing exposure to the Party’s advertisement, labeling Trump as a “convicted criminal” (n = 814) relative to no advertisement (n = 813), on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with records",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "claim": "The study also seeks to understand if providing a perspective from a formerly system-involved representative can counteract the negative impacts of the stigmatizing advertisement.",
            "quotes": [
              {
                "text": "Additionally, this study seeks to understand whether providing a counterexample to Trump – highlighting the perspective of a formerly system-involved representative on the propagation of this label by the Democratic party – may counter-act potential negative impacts of the Party’s Trump advertisement.",
                "page": 4
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Exposure to a Democratic ad labeling Trump as a 'convicted criminal' significantly worsens Democrats' perceptions of individuals with criminal records, but presenting a counter-narrative featuring a formerly incarcerated representative before the ad largely ameliorates these negative effects.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Results suggest that exposure to the Trump advertisement with the “convicted criminal” label had significant, negative impacts on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with records... Findings suggest that the counter-stereotype vastly ameliorated the negative effects of the advertisement on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with criminal records.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "First, there is clear evidence that the Trump advertisement caused decreases in positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records amongst Democrats, but when paired with (and preceded by) a counter-narrative of a dedicated, system-impacted state representative who speaks to the harm of the “criminal” label, these effects were almost entirely ameliorated",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The study uses a survey experiment conducted via Prolific with 2,439 self-identified Democrats, randomly assigning them to three groups: Control, Trump Ad Alone, or Counter-Narrative First.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "this preliminary study employs a survey experiment testing exposure to the Party’s advertisement, labeling Trump as a “convicted criminal” (n = 814) relative to no advertisement (n = 813), on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with records",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "Thus, the 2,439 participants were allocated approximately evenly across one of three different treatment groups: a) the Control group (n = 813); b) the Trump Ad Alone group (n = 814); and c) the Counter-Narrative First group (n = 812).",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The stimuli included an actual image used in the 2024 election showing Trump's mugshot labeled 'CONVICTED CRIMINAL' and a text excerpt from an article about Jovan Jackson, a formerly incarcerated Nevada assemblymember.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Participants were presented with one of the images utilized in advertisements within the 2024 presidential election, specifically President Donald J. Trump’s mugshot, which was labeled, in all capital letters, “CONVICTED CRIMINAL.”",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "This excerpt was from the article entitled, “Could a formerly incarcerated lawmaker reshape justice policy in the NV Legislature?,” by Michael Lyle, published in the Nevada Current on November 5, 2024",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Exposure to the Trump advertisement alone significantly decreased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records among Democrats compared to the control group.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "exposure to the Trump advertisement alone significantly decreased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records ( β̂0 = −0.835, SE = 0.044)",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Presenting a counter-narrative featuring a formerly incarcerated representative prior to the advertisement significantly increased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records, effectively offsetting the negative impact of the ad.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "exposure to the counter-narrative prior to being presented with the Trump advertisement increased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records by more than 0.8 standard deviation (SD) units, compared to those presented with the Trump advertisement alone ( β̂2 = 0.83, SE = 0.041).",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Exposure to the Trump advertisement improved perceived feelings of representation and beliefs that elected officials are motivated by public service, likely due to Trump serving as a low benchmark for comparison.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "exposure to the Trump advertisement significantly increased feelings of being well represented by elected officials and the extent to which Democrats perceived that elected officials were motivated by public service over self-interest.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "Donald J. Trump may be serving as reference point that may influence Democrats to see other elected officials as comparatively better",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "System-impacted individuals (those with personal connections to the justice system) reported more positive perceptions of people with criminal records overall, and the negative effects of the advertisement were more damaging for this group.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "those who identified as being system-impacted reported significantly more positive perceptions overall and within each treatment arm, with only one exception.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "the Trump advertisement exposure appeared to be significantly more damaging to perceptions of individuals with criminal records for those who are system-impacted.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The study lacks a full factorial design, meaning it cannot isolate the specific effect of the counter-narrative alone without the advertisement, nor can it disentangle the impact of the mugshot image from the stigmatizing text.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "failing to include the counter-narrative only arm limits the ability to interpret the additional impact of including it with the advertisements; in other words, the counter-narrative arm cannot be directly compared to the pure control arm to test the impact of the counter-narrative alone",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "it is not possible to disentangle the impact of the image from the stigmatizing terminology imposed, and thus, this is recommended for exploration in future work.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The results are based on a single time-point online survey, leaving open questions about the durability of effects, replication in field settings during active campaigns, and the potential influence of generative AI bots on data quality.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the proliferation of generative AI should cause concerns over the humanity of the responses provided within the survey.",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "leaves unanswered several key questions related to the ability of these results to replicate: a) for how long do the effects of the counter-narrative prime before the advertisement and the advertisement itself persist",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The author positions the study within social categorization theory, arguing that salient group exemplars (like Trump) lead to homogenized perceptions of the broader group (individuals with records), thereby extending this theory to explain political messaging spillover.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Leaning upon social categorization theory, propagating such labels on Trump may thereby spillover, impacting constituents’ perspectives of individuals with records more broadly.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "theory and evidence would predict greater homogenization and spillover perceptions from Trump, such that perceptions of individuals with records would be worsened amongst Democrats after exposure to the advertisement",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "The paper disputes the strategic efficacy of negative campaigning, citing meta-analytic evidence that such ads do not aid election success, and contrasts this with the potential benefits of humanizing, perspective-getting narratives.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "meta-analytic evidence does not empirically support the use of negative campaign advertisements to aid election success",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "utilizing political messaging that elevates dedicated, justice-involved voices within the party rather than rely on stigmatizing attacks should be strongly considered and tested.",
            "page": 1
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
